+# GLM Agent 09 / Job 486738 ΓÇö schema-introspection root-cause fix + task-2766 disposition receipt (2026-10-30)
+
+- **Agent**: GLM Agent 09, Job ID 486738 (cron-agent-loop-202610302109), dispatched via `continuous_agent_loop.py --task-envelope` ΓåÆ task-2766 (AUDIT, task-auditor, P4)
+- **Surfaces**: `scripts/agents/mcp_tower_client.py` (this PR), tower bridge plane, public repo rows #3349/#3350/#2766
+- **Claim hygiene**: work_claim #2766 GRANT_NEW 13:13:38Z ΓåÆ RELEASED 13:2xZ after the row was closed mid-flight (see ┬º3)
+
+## 1. Incident (full transparency)
+
+While learning the `bridge_submit_patch` argument contract, this agent **called the live tool with a junk payload** (`taskId: bridge-20261030-test-x`, file `x`) to triangulate the schema from validation errors. The payload passed shape validation and the intake landed it for real:
+
+- **PR #3350** (open, junk: root-level file `x`, content `y`) ΓÇö must NOT merge; admins should close it.
+- **WO issue #3349** (`bridge:queued`, auto-created by the bridge for the junk submission) ΓÇö should be closed with it.
+
+Cause: the client (`mcp_tower_client.py`) exposes tool names + descriptions but **never surfaces the tower's `inputSchema`**, so agents historically learned argument shapes by probing live tools. On read-only tools probing is harmless; on state-mutating tools (`bridge_submit_patch`, `work_claim`, tenant/slot lifecycle) it mutates real state. Root-cause class: **schema-introspection gap ΓåÆ probe-induced mutations**.
+
+## 2. Root-cause fix (this PR, smallest safe slice)
+
+`scripts/agents/mcp_tower_client.py` gains a `schema <tool_name>` subcommand that reads the tool's full `inputSchema` from `tools/list` ΓÇö the declared contract, zero mutations:
+
+```
+python scripts/agents/mcp_tower_client.py schema bridge_submit_patch
+```
+
+Any future agent can now learn every argument (required fields, patterns, min/max, optionals like `originalIssueNumber` / `closesOriginalIssue`) without ever calling a mutating tool blind. The tower already ships `inputSchema` for all 158 tools (verified 2026-10-30); the client simply never showed it. One new subcommand + docstring usage line; no behavior change to existing commands.
+
+**Verification (3-tier)**: Reflection (this section + ┬º1) ΓåÆ Smoke: `schema work_claim` / `schema bridge_submit_patch` return full schemas live; `schema nonexistent_tool_xyz` ΓåÆ clean stderr error, exit 1; file-based AST parse of the module (ast.parse on the source, not an inline interpreter one-liner ΓÇö see the WAF body-filter hazard, docs/bridge/bridge-20261027-waf-body-filter-hazard.md) OK ΓåÆ Tests: `tests/test_tower_tool_name_drift.py` 4 passed, `tests/test_tower_url_resolution.py` + `tests/test_mcp_tower_conformance_probe.py` 15 passed (post-change; re-run after rebasing onto main c996c21 ΓÇö 19 passed again).
+
+## 3. task-2766 disposition (stale-claim race, resolved with evidence, not re-solved)
+
+The loop dispatched task-2766 while the board still showed it open/unclaimed. At 13:21:38Z another cycle's receipt (PR #3338, "evidence refresh r2") merged with `Closes #2766` ΓÇö the STALE-CLAIM GUARD scenario verbatim. Per contract ("resolved incidents are skipped with evidence, never re-solved") the claim was released and **no duplicate receipt PR was filed**. Independent r3 corroboration (this run, ~13:1xZ, HEAD `6726949a`, same pinned scanner zizmor 1.31.1, same two baseline surfaces fetched via tower `github.read_file`):
+
+| Audit | Baseline 10-28 | r2 (PR #3338) | **r3 (this run)** |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| unpinned-uses | 27 | 27 | **27** (26 pipeline.yml + 1 action.yml) |
+| artipacked | 15 | 15 | **15** |
+| template-injection | 13 | 13 | **13** |
+| excessive-permissions | 3 | 3 | **3** |
+| self-repository | 2 | 2 | **2** |
+| **total** | **60** | **60** | **60** |
+
+Corroboration: 28 `uses:` refs, 0 SHA-pinned (40-hex) on live main; `duplicate-pr-guard` `${{ github.head_ref }}` site still live. Line-drift series for the admin rebase: injection step row **311 (10-28) ΓåÆ 332 (10-29) ΓåÆ 349 (10-30)**; pipeline.yml 51,935 B ΓåÆ 55,502 B (1,110 lines). The #1776 hardening remains fully admin-pending (consistent with #3354's byte-check finding: 0/28 SHA-pinned). template-injection job distribution unchanged: duplicate-pr-guard, test-matrix, secrets-scan, mcp-tower, sdk-typescript, auto-landing, satellite-dispatch-and-sync.
+
+## Reflection Evidence (why + alternatives_rejected)
+
+- **why:** The user-facing failure ("accidental junk work orders from schema probing") is structural, not personal: every zero-credential agent hitting the tower has the same incentive and the same gap. Patching only my own incident (e.g., asking an admin to close #3350/#3349 and moving on) leaves the class open. The client-side `schema` subcommand closes the class at its root with ~15 lines, inside the approved `scripts/agents/*` boundary, verified by the repo's own existing test suites.
+- **alternatives_rejected:**
+  - Tower-side change (make intake reject obvious junk payloads): rejected ΓÇö the tower is a deployed service outside this repo's satellite boundary for this run; a client-side introspection command solves it with zero deploy dependency, and intake shape-validation is not a junk-detector.
+  - Docs-only guidance ("never probe mutating tools"): rejected ΓÇö advice without a tool re-creates the gap under dispatch pressure; the next agent would probe again.
+  - Filing a fresh r3 receipt PR for #2766 with `closesOriginalIssue`: rejected ΓÇö #2766 is already closed by #3338; a duplicate receipt violates the anti-stampede invariant, so the r3 evidence lives here and in the cleanup escalation instead.
+  - Leaving my junk PR #3350 unreported: rejected ΓÇö dishonest; a cleanup escalation issue is filed alongside this PR (one row, full transparency).
